main/access-modifiers.html.description:
The member or <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> can be accessed from any package, as long as it is visible through the package/module setup.
<br>For <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Class" data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">classes</a>: <br>A top-level public <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> must have the same name as the source file. public class Student {
}
File name:Student.java The member can be accessed: <br>From <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Class" data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">classes</a> in the same package.
From subclasses, even if the subclass is in a different package. protected is generally used for members that should be accessible to subclasses.
<br>A top-level <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> cannot be protected. Package-Private <br>If no access modifier is specified, the member or <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> has package-private access.
It can only be accessed from code in the same package.
This is the default access level when no modifier is specified.
<br>A top-level <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> can be package-private.
class Student {
}
<br>A <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> in a different package cannot directly access Student.
<br>The member can only be accessed within the <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> where it is declared.
<br>private is commonly used to hide a <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a>'s internal data and implementation.
<br>A top-level <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> cannot be private.
class Student { private String name;
}
<br>
Note: Top-level <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Class" data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">classes</a> can only be public or package-private. They cannot be private or protected.

main/casting-&-conversion-on-assignment..description:
Casting is a conversion where the operand expression needs to be converted to the type specified by the cast operator.(targetType) expression
Example:double num = 10.5;
int x = (int) num;
The double value is explicitly converted to int.Java includes several conversion processes:
The type does not actually change.
The expression is already the same type as the target type.
int x = 10;
int y = x;
int → int
Converts a primitive type into another primitive type that can represent a larger range of values.
Usually performed automatically.
No explicit cast is required.
Common examples:byte → short → int → long → float → double char → int → long → float → double
Example:int x = 10;
double y = x;
int → double
Converts a primitive type into another primitive type with a smaller range or precision.
Usually requires an explicit cast.
May result in loss of data or precision.
Example:double x = 10.5;
int y = (int) x;
Result:10.5 → 10
The decimal portion is lost.
Converts a reference to a subclass into a reference to one of its superclasses.
Usually performed automatically.
It is also known as upcasting.
// ClassCastException at runtime

