main/java-records.html.description:
Java records are a concise way to create classes that mainly store data.
Records automatically provide: Constructor
Getters/accessor methods
toString()
equals()
hashCode() Records are immutable by default.
Record fields cannot be changed after the object is created.
record Person(String name, int age) {
} Person = record name
name and age = record components
Person person = new Person("John", 25); Records don't use traditional getters like getName().
Use the component name as a method:
System.out.println(person.name()); // John
System.out.println(person.age()); // 25
Person person = new Person("John", 25); // Cannot do:
// person.name = "Bob";
// person.setName("Bob"); If you need different values, create a new record:
person = new Person("Bob", 30);
The constructor is automatically created based on the record components.record Person(String name, int age) {
} Person person = new Person("John", 25); Equivalent constructor:
new Person(String name, int age) You can add validation using a compact constructor:
record Person(String name, int age) { Person { if (age &lt; 0) { throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative"); } }
}
You can add your own methods to a record.record Person(String name, int age) { boolean isAdult() { return age &gt;= 18; }
}
Usage:Person person = new Person("John", 25); System.out.println(person.isAdult()); // true
record Student( String name, int age, double grade
) {
}
Create:Student student = new Student("Alice", 20, 95.5);
Access:System.out.println(student.name());
System.out.println(student.age());
System.out.println(student.grade());
Records can implement interfaces.interface Printable { void print();
} record Person(String name, int age) implements Printable { @Override public void print() { System.out.println(name + " - " + age); }
}
Usage:Person person = new Person("John", 25); person.print();

main/non-access-modifiers.html.description:
A final <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> cannot be subclassed.
<br>It is used when a <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> is designed so that it must not be extended.
<br><a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Methods</a> in a final <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> cannot be overridden because the <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> itself cannot be subclassed.
Commonly used in core libraries and APIs when the implementation should not be changed through inheritance.
public final class Student {
}
<br>
Note: final does not mean that the object's state cannot change. It only prevents inheritance when applied to a <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a>. <br>
An abstract <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> cannot be instantiated directly. It is designed to be extended by subclasses. <br>
An abstract <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> can contain: <br>Abstract <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">methods</a>, which have no implementation.
<br>Concrete <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">methods</a>, which have an implementation.
Fields and constructors. <br>
A subclass must implement all inherited abstract <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">methods</a> unless the subclass is also abstract. public abstract class Animal { abstract void makeSound(); void sleep() { System.out.println("Sleeping..."); }
} transient is used with instance fields.
<br>A transient field is excluded from Java's default <a data-href="Serialization" href="main/serialization.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Serialization</a>.
When an object is deserialized, a transient field receives its default value (null, 0, false, etc.).
class User implements Serializable { String username; transient String password;
} Note: transient does not prevent the field from existing in memory. It only affects serialization. <br>synchronized is used with <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">methods</a> or blocks.
It allows only one thread at a time to execute the synchronized code for a given lock.
It is commonly used to protect shared mutable data from race conditions.
public synchronized void increment() { count++;
}
<br>
see: <a data-href="Native Method Process" href="main/native-method-process.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Native Method Process</a> <br>native indicates that a <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">method</a> is implemented outside of Java, typically in a language such as C or C++.
<br>The <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">method</a> has no Java implementation body.
<br>Native <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">methods</a> are commonly used when Java needs to interact with operating-system or low-level functionality.
public native void someMethod();
<br>
Note: A native <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">method</a> must normally be implemented through the Java Native Interface (JNI) or another supported native mechanism. strictfp was used to require strict floating-point calculations according to Java's floating-point rules.
Since Java 17, floating-point expressions are strict by default.
Therefore, strictfp is obsolete and should generally not be used in new code.
public strictfp class Calculator {
} Note: strictfp is retained for compatibility, but the compiler may issue a warning that it is unnecessary. <br>
static indicates that a member belongs to the <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> rather than to individual objects. <br>
A static member can be accessed without creating an instance of the <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a>. static can be used with: Fields <br>
<a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Java Methods and Functions" data-href="Java Methods and Functions" href="main/java-methods-and-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Methods</a> <br>
Nested <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Class" data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">classes</a> Initialization blocks class Student { static int count = 0; static void showCount() { System.out.println(count); }
}
Access:Student.showCount();
<br>
Note: A top-level <a data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Class</a> cannot be static. Only nested <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Class" data-href="Class" href="main/class.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">classes</a> can be declared static. volatile is used with instance or static fields.
It tells the JVM that reads and writes to the field should be handled with thread visibility in mind.
<br>When one thread changes a volatile <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Variables" data-href="Variables" href="main/variables.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">variable</a>, other threads can see the updated value without relying on a thread's local cached copy.
volatile does not make compound operations such as count++ atomic.
class Counter { private volatile boolean running = true;
} Note: volatile provides visibility guarantees, but it does not replace synchronized when multiple operations need to be performed atomically.

