main/regex.html.description:
Java provides the Pattern and Matcher classes in the java.util.regex package for working with Regular Expressions (Regex).
represents a compiled regular expression.
Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("abc"); Pattern.compile() compiles the regex into a pattern structure.
The compiled pattern can then be used to create a Matcher. is used to match a compiled Pattern against a sequence of characters.
Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("abc");
Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher("abc");
Meta characters are special characters in regex that have a specific meaning.
In Java strings, a backslash must usually be escaped. Therefore regex \d is written as Java string "\\d". parentheses () create a capturing group.
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("(\\w+)@(\\w+\\.com)");
Matcher m = p.matcher("john@gmail.com");
There are two capturing groups:(\\w+) → Group 1 → "john"
(\\w+\\.com) → Group 2 → "gmail.com"
Groups are numbered from 1.$1 → first capturing group
$2 → second capturing group
$3 → third capturing group
... Does the whole string match
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\d+"); Matcher m = p.matcher("12345"); System.out.println(m.matches()); // true Matcher m = p.matcher("abc123"); System.out.println(m.matches()); // false searches for the next substring that matches the pattern.
unlike matches(), find() does not require the entire input to match.
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\d+");
Matcher m = p.matcher("abc123xyz456"); while (m.find()) { System.out.println(m.group());
}
Output:123
456
After a successful find(), you can use:
start() → starting index of the match
end() → index immediately after the match
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\d+");
Matcher m = p.matcher("abc123xyz"); if (m.find()) { System.out.println(m.start()); System.out.println(m.end()); System.out.println(m.group());
}
Output:3
6
123 replaces every substring that matches the pattern.
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\d+");
Matcher m = p.matcher("Java 123 is 456"); String result = m.replaceAll("#"); System.out.println(result);
Output:Java # is # $1 is used in a replacement string to refer to the text captured by the first capturing group.
Example:
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("(\\w+)@(\\w+\\.com)");
Matcher m = p.matcher("john@gmail.com"); String result = m.replaceAll("$2 : $1"); System.out.println(result);
Output:gmail.com : john
The pattern captures:Group 1 → john
Group 2 → gmail.com
Note
$0 --&gt; refers to the whole match instead of just one group used to get the actual text that was matched.
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\d+");
Matcher m = p.matcher("abc123xyz"); if (m.find()) { System.out.println(m.group());
}
Output:123
So:m.group()
→ returns the entire match
You can also explicitly write:m.group(0)
This means the same thing:group() = group(0) = entire match return the text captured by the corresponding (...) group.
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("(\\w+)@(\\w+\\.com)");
Matcher m = p.matcher("john@gmail.com"); if (m.find()) { System.out.println(m.group(0)); System.out.println(m.group(1)); System.out.println(m.group(2));
}
Output:john@gmail.com
john
gmail.com occurs when Java cannot compile an invalid regular expression.
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("[abc"); try { Pattern p = Pattern.compile("[abc");
} catch (PatternSyntaxException e) { System.out.println("Invalid regex pattern");
}
Key idea
Pattern → defines WHAT to look for
Matcher → searches WHERE and HOW it matches

