<br>Modern replacement for <a data-href="SimpleDateFormat" href="main/simpledateformat.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">SimpleDateFormat</a>
<br><a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Mutability" data-href="Mutability" href="main/mutability.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Immutable</a> and thread-safe
DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ISO_LOCAL_DATE; LocalDate date = LocalDate.parse("2026-09-09", formatter); Provides predefined formats such as: ISO_LOCAL_DATE
ISO_LOCAL_TIME
ISO_LOCAL_DATE_TIME
ISO_ZONED_DATE_TIME DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd"); String result = LocalDate.now().format(formatter);
DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern( "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss" ); String result = LocalDateTime.now().format(formatter);
DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd"); LocalDate date = LocalDate.parse("2026-09-09", formatter); format() → date/time → String
parse() → String → date/time
DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern( "dd MMMM yyyy", Locale.US ); Locale controls language-specific text such as month and day names. Validating user input
String rulePattern = "MMMM dd, yyyy";
DateTimeFormatter parser = new DateTimeFormatter(rulePattern); // checks if value adheres to pattern
try { LocalDate bday = LocalDate.from(parser.parse(input.strip())); System.out.println(bday);
} catch (DateTimeParseException e) { System.err.println("Invalid Input...");
} <br>Note
Unlike <a data-href="SimpleDateFormat" href="main/simpledateformat.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">SimpleDateFormat</a>, DateTimeFormatter is thread-safe, making it safe to reuse as a shared formatter.

main/decimalformat.html.description:
Concrete subclass of <a data-href="NumberFormat" href="main/numberformat.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">NumberFormat</a>
Formats and parses decimal numbers using patterns
Useful when you need precise control over the number's display format
DecimalFormat df = new DecimalFormat("#,##0.00"); System.out.println(df.format(1234567.8));
// 1,234,567.80 0 → required digit; displays 0 if missing
# → optional digit; does not display unnecessary 0
. → decimal separator
, → grouping separator
% → formats as percentage
- → negative sign Pattern can be used as an input mask to control the expected number format
0 forces digits, while # allows optional digits
DecimalFormat df = new DecimalFormat("000-000"); System.out.println(df.format(123));
// 000-123 Validating user input
String rulePattern = "\u20B1###,###.00";
DecimalFormat parser = new DecimalFormat(rulePattern); // checks if value adheres to pattern
try { // double salary = parser.parse(input).doubleValue(); BigDecimal salary = new BigDecimal(parser.parse(input).toString()); System.out.println(salary);
} catch (ParseException e) { System.err.println("Invalid Input...");
} Note
DecimalFormat patterns are primarily formatting patterns, not full validation masks. For strict user-input validation, additional validation may be needed. format() → formats a number into a String
parse() → parses a String into a Number
applyPattern() → changes the formatting pattern
toPattern() → returns the current pattern
setMinimumFractionDigits() → sets minimum decimal places
setMaximumFractionDigits() → sets maximum decimal places
<br>setRoundingMode() → sets the <a data-href="Rounding Mode" href="main/rounding-mode.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Rounding Mode</a>

main/numberformat.html.description:
Abstract class for formatting and parsing numbers
Formats numbers according to locale-specific rules
Commonly used for decimal numbers, percentages, and currencies
NumberFormat nf = NumberFormat.getNumberInstance(); System.out.println(nf.format(1234567.89));
// 1,234,567.89
NumberFormat nf = NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance(); System.out.println(nf.format(1234.50));
// $1,234.50
NumberFormat nf = NumberFormat.getPercentInstance(); System.out.println(nf.format(0.75));
// 75% format() → converts a number into a formatted String
parse() → converts a String into a Number
setMinimumFractionDigits() → sets minimum decimal places
setMaximumFractionDigits() → sets maximum decimal places
setRoundingMode() → sets the <a data-href="Rounding Mode" href="main/rounding-mode.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Rounding Mode</a>
setGroupingUsed() → enables/disables grouping separators
setCurrency() → sets the currency
NumberFormat nf = NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance( Locale.US ); <br>Formatting depends on the <a data-href="Locale" href="main/locale.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Locale</a>
Different locales use different symbols and separators
<br>Note
NumberFormat is primarily for display formatting. For exact decimal calculations, use <a data-href="BigDecimal" href="main/bigdecimal.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">BigDecimal</a>.
